# 📐 of 🌲 and of 🍪: a type method of a class reads the generic arguments of its class from the type value, where they
# follow the class, and takes no other parameter for them.
@ ^_\.class_1f332\.type\.1f4d0$
+ %typeGenericArgs = getelementptr inbounds %typeDescription, ptr %this, i32 1
@ ^_\.class_1f36a\.type\.1f4d0$
+ %typeGenericArgs = getelementptr inbounds %typeDescription, ptr %this, i32 1
# 🥩 and 🥐 of 🔍: the type methods of 🅰️ and 🍩, the latter without generic parameters, are called through the type
# value alike, which is the only argument.
@ ^_\.class_1f50d\.type\.1f969-
+ call ptr %dispatchFunc\(ptr %a\)
- ejcTypeValue
@ ^_\.class_1f50d\.type\.1f950-
+ call ptr %dispatchFunc\(ptr %a\)
# 🌿 and 🍂 of 🔍: a type value described at run time is copied to one that lives as long as the program.
@ ^_\.class_1f50d\.type\.1f33f$
+ call ptr @ejcTypeValue
@ ^_\.class_1f50d\.type\.1f342$
+ call ptr @ejcTypeValue
# 🏁: the type values of classes with known generic arguments are constants.
@ ^fn_1f3c1$
- ejcTypeValue
